<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code code={`abide call invoices/getInvoice id=4310`} />

    <p>An argument is <code>name=value</code>, and a name given twice is a list. The value is text
    until the handler's schema says otherwise, so <code>id=4310</code> arrives as a number where
    <code>id</code> is declared one — the same reading a <code>GET</code>'s query string gets
    (38.61). An argument shape one word per name cannot spell, an object inside an object, is typed
    as one JSON object instead:</p>

    <Code code={`abide call invoices/createInvoice '{"customer": {"id": "c-9"}, "lines": []}'`} />

    <Section title="The schema is the interface">
        <p>A handler's argument shape is what the app holds the call to, so a mistyped argument is
        refused with a message naming the field, and <code>call</code> exits 7. That is the same
        schema the browser wrapper is built from and the same one OpenAPI publishes; there is one
        per handler and the CLI is a reader of it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/record-what-happened')}>Logging</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Exit codes tell a refusal from a fault">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>the handler answered</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>7</code></td><td>the handler <strong>refused</strong></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>8</code></td><td>the call reached the app and the app faulted</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A refusal is an expected answer, so it is not a failure of the CLI. A script can branch
        on those two without parsing anything, which is the difference between a deploy check that
        can act on "not yours" and one that can only retry.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`abide` alone is the interactive form">
        <Code code={`abide                              # the built app, served, and a shell on it
abide --url https://ledger.example # a shell on a running app`} />

        <p>A shell on the app: the handlers are in scope, the completions come from the same route
        table, and a call is a call. <code>help</code> lists what it takes and <code>handlers</code>
        every address; Tab completes an address or an argument name, and the handler's arguments are
        shown as you type. Ctrl+C clears the line, or cancels a call still running. It is for the
        questions that are one-off by nature — what does this record look like, does this handler
        still refuse that — where writing a script is more ceremony than the question deserves.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Which app, and with what">
        <p><code>--url</code>, then <code>ABIDE_APP_URL</code>, then the local app.
        <code>ABIDE_APP_TOKEN</code> is the bearer sent with it, so a remote app is reachable
        without a second set of credentials to manage.</p>

        <p>Both are ordinary environment variables, which means a shell profile per environment is
        the whole of the setup.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/reference/configuration')}>Configuration</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/watch-a-room-from-the-terminal')}>Watch a channel</a> — the same
            connection, for a channel</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/cli')}>CLI</a> — every command and flag</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
